CS 214 Spring 2026 - Project IV: A Simple Chat Server
Authors: Haaris Toor (hbt20), Hassan Ibrahim (hi125)

OVERVIEW
========
This project implements a simple Internet chat server (chatd) that supports
multiple clients connecting simultaneously. The server forwards messages between
clients, manages unique screen names, and handles user queries.

FEATURES
========
- Multi-client support using multithreading/poll
- User authentication with unique screen names
- Room-based messaging (default room: #all)
- Private messaging between users
- User status management
- User listing and queries
- Error handling and validation

PROTOCOL
========
The server implements a text-based protocol with the following message types:
- NAM: Set user screen name
- SET: Set user status
- MSG: Send/receive messages
- WHO: Query user information
- ERR: Error responses

Message Format: VERSION|CODE|LENGTH|FIELDS...|

Example messages:
- NAM: 1|NAM|4|Bob|
- MSG: 1|MSG|23|Bob|#all|Hello, world!|
- WHO: 1|WHO|5|#all|
- ERR: 1|ERR|15|1|Name in use|

BUILDING
========
To compile the server and test client:
    make

To clean build artifacts:
    make clean

USAGE
=====
    ./chatd <port>

Where <port> is the port number on which the server will listen for connections.

Example:
    ./chatd 8080

To test with the included test client:
    ./test_client localhost 8080 YourName

IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
====================
- Uses multithreading for handling multiple clients
- Mutex locks protect shared data structures
- Screen names: 1-32 characters (letters, digits, hyphen, underscore)
- Status messages: 0-64 characters (ASCII 32-126)
- Chat messages: 1-80 characters (ASCII 32-126)

PROJECT STRUCTURE
=================
Core Files:
- chatd.c       - Main server implementation with threading support
- protocol.h    - Protocol definitions and function prototypes
- protocol.c    - Protocol parsing and message formatting functions
- Makefile      - Build system

Testing Files:
- test_client.c - Simple interactive test client
- test.sh       - Bash script for automated testing

IMPLEMENTATION GUIDE
====================
The skeleton code contains TODO comments marking what needs to be implemented.

Priority 1: Protocol Parsing (protocol.c)
------------------------------------------
Complete the parse_message() function:
1. Use strtok() or manual parsing to extract fields separated by '|'
2. Extract version field and verify it equals "1"
3. Extract code field (3 characters: NAM, SET, MSG, WHO, ERR)
4. Extract length field (up to 5 digits, convert to integer)
5. Read exactly 'length' bytes for the body
6. Verify the body ends with '|'
7. Store parsed data in message_t structure
8. Return 0 on success, -1 on error

Hints:
- Use strdup() to allocate memory for the body
- Be careful about null terminators vs actual message content
- The length field counts bytes in the body INCLUDING the final '|'

Priority 2: Client Message Loop (chatd.c)
------------------------------------------
In handle_client(), implement the main message loop:
1. Read data from socket into buffer (handle partial reads!)
2. Call parse_message() to parse the incoming message
3. Check message code and route to appropriate handler:
   - "NAM" -> handle_nam_message()
   - "SET" -> handle_set_message()
   - "MSG" -> handle_msg_message()
   - "WHO" -> handle_who_message()
4. On parse error, send error 0 (unreadable) and close connection
5. Call free_message() to clean up after each message
6. Handle client disconnect gracefully

Important: Clients must send NAM before any other message!

Priority 3: NAM Handler (chatd.c)
----------------------------------
Implement handle_nam_message():
1. Extract screen name from body (remove trailing '|')
2. Call validate_screen_name() to check format
3. If invalid format: send error 3 (illegal character) or 4 (too long)
4. Call find_client_by_name() to check if name already in use
5. If in use: send error 1 (name in use)
6. If valid and unique:
   - Copy screen name to client->screen_name
   - Set client->authenticated = 1
   - Call add_client() to add to global list
   - Use create_msg_message() to send welcome from #all
7. Example: create_msg_message("#all", screen_name, "Welcome to the chat!")

Priority 4: MSG Handler (chatd.c)
----------------------------------
Implement handle_msg_message():
1. Parse body to extract three fields: sender|recipient|text|
   (Use strtok with '|' delimiter)
2. IMPORTANT: Ignore the sender field sent by client (prevent spoofing!)
3. Use client->screen_name as the actual sender
4. Validate message text with validate_message_text()
5. If invalid: send error 3 or 4
6. If recipient is "#all":
   - Use broadcast_message() to send to all authenticated clients
   - Create message with create_msg_message(client->screen_name, "#all", text)
7. If recipient is specific user:
   - Use find_client_by_name() to locate recipient
   - If not found: send error 2 (unknown recipient)
   - If found: use send_to_client() to send only to that user
   - Create message with create_msg_message(client->screen_name, recipient, text)

Priority 5: SET Handler (chatd.c)
----------------------------------
Implement handle_set_message():
1. Extract status from body (remove trailing '|')
2. Call validate_status() to check format
3. If invalid: send error 3 or 4
4. Copy status to client->status
5. If status is non-empty:
   - Create message like "Bob is now \"Smiling politely\""
   - Use create_msg_message("#all", "#all", formatted_message)
   - Call broadcast_message() to notify all users

Priority 6: WHO Handler (chatd.c)
----------------------------------
Implement handle_who_message():
1. Extract target from body (remove trailing '|')
2. If target is "#all":
   - Lock clients_mutex
   - Iterate through all authenticated clients
   - Build response string with format: "name: status\nname2: status2\nname3"
   - Users with no status: just print the name
   - Unlock mutex
3. If target is specific user:
   - Use find_client_by_name() to find user
   - If not found: send error 2 (unknown recipient)
   - If found: format as "name: status" or "No status"
4. Send response using create_msg_message("#all", client->screen_name, response)

Priority 7: Error Handling
---------------------------
Implement error responses throughout:
- Use create_err_message(code, explanation) to create error messages
- Error 0 is FATAL: after sending, close connection and return
- Errors 1-4 are recoverable: send error and continue
- Always validate user input before processing

COMMON PITFALLS
===============
1. Buffer overflows: Always validate lengths before copying strings
2. Race conditions: Always lock clients_mutex before accessing client list
3. Partial reads: Socket recv() may not receive full message at once
4. Memory leaks: Free allocated messages with free_message() and free()
5. Error 0 vs others: Only error 0 should close the connection
6. Sender spoofing: Always override sender field with client->screen_name
7. Deadlocks: Be careful with mutex lock ordering

DEVELOPMENT TIPS
================
1. Start with parse_message() - everything else depends on it
2. Test NAM and basic connection handling first
3. Test with single client before testing multiple clients
4. Use telnet or netcat for manual protocol testing:
   telnet localhost 8080
   1|NAM|4|Bob|
5. Add debug printf statements to trace message flow
6. Test error cases systematically
7. Use valgrind to check for memory leaks:
   valgrind --leak-check=full ./chatd 8080
8. The test_client.c provides a working example of the protocol

QUICK START
===========
1. Read through all the skeleton code files
2. Understand the protocol format from the project spec
3. Implement parse_message() in protocol.c (needed by everything)
4. Implement the handle_client() message loop in chatd.c
5. Implement handlers one at a time, testing after each:
   - handle_nam_message()
   - handle_msg_message()
   - handle_set_message()
   - handle_who_message()
6. Build comprehensive test cases
7. Test edge cases and error conditions
8. Run valgrind to check for memory leaks

TEST PLAN
=========
1. Connection Testing
   - Test multiple clients connecting simultaneously
   - Test client disconnection and reconnection
   - Test maximum connections

2. Screen Name Testing
   - Test valid screen names (letters, digits, hyphen, underscore)
   - Test invalid characters in screen names
   - Test duplicate screen names
   - Test screen name length limits (1-32 characters)

3. Message Broadcasting
   - Test messages sent to #all room
   - Verify all connected users receive broadcast messages
   - Test message length limits (1-80 characters)
   - Test invalid characters in messages

4. Private Messaging
   - Test sending private messages to specific users
   - Test sending to non-existent users (should error)
   - Verify only intended recipient receives message

5. Status Management
   - Test setting user status
   - Test status length limits (0-64 characters)
   - Test status character validation
   - Verify status broadcasts to #all

6. User Queries (WHO)
   - Test querying specific user information
   - Test querying all users in #all
   - Test querying non-existent users
   - Verify response format

7. Error Handling
   - Test error 0: Unreadable/malformed messages
   - Test error 1: Name in use
   - Test error 2: Unknown recipient
   - Test error 3: Illegal character
   - Test error 4: Too long
   - Verify connection closed after error 0

8. Protocol Compliance
   - Test message format parsing
   - Test protocol version validation
   - Test message length field accuracy
   - Test proper message termination with |

9. Stress Testing
   - Test rapid message sending
   - Test many simultaneous connections
   - Test long-running sessions

10. Edge Cases
    - Test empty fields where applicable
    - Test boundary values for lengths
    - Test special characters within allowed ranges
    - Test client disconnect during message transmission

Testing Tools:
- Custom test clients using sockets
- telnet for manual protocol testing
- Automated test scripts for regression testing
